const a=[{slug:"sublimation-vs-silk-screen",title:"Choosing between sublimation and silk screen",date:"12 Jun 2026",iso:"2026-06-12",category:"Guides",excerpt:"How fabric type, colour count, and order quantity decide which decoration method gives you the best result.",readingTime:"4 min read",body:[{type:"p",text:"Most decoration decisions come down to three questions: what is the garment made of, how many colours are in the artwork, and how many pieces are you making. Answer those honestly and the method usually picks itself."},{type:"h",text:"Start with the fabric"},{type:"p",text:"Sublimation is a chemical process, not a coating. Under heat the dye turns to gas and bonds with polyester fibres, becoming part of the fabric rather than sitting on top of it. That is why a sublimated jersey never cracks or peels, and why it will not work on cotton — there is nothing in a cotton fibre for the dye to bond to."},{type:"p",text:"Silk screen is the opposite: ink pushed through a mesh stencil and cured onto the surface. It works on cotton, polyester, and blends, which makes it the more flexible option when the fabric is already decided."},{type:"note",text:"If the garment is 100% cotton, sublimation is off the table before you get to any other question."},{type:"h",text:"Then count the colours"},{type:"p",text:"Silk screen prices per colour, because each one needs its own screen, its own setup, and its own pass. A two-colour chest print is inexpensive. A photographic gradient is not — it may need six or seven screens and still not resolve cleanly."},{type:"p",text:"Sublimation does not care. A full-coverage design with a hundred colours costs the same as a single-colour one, because it is one transfer either way. Anything with photographic imagery, gradients, or all-over panels points to sublimation."},{type:"h",text:"Finally, the quantity"},{type:"ul",items:["Silk screen carries a fixed setup cost per colour, so unit price falls sharply as volume rises. It is usually the cheapest option at scale for simple artwork.","Sublimation has almost no setup, so the per-unit price stays flat. That makes it the better economics on small runs, and the only sane choice when every piece is different — names and numbers, for instance.","For short runs on cotton, DTF or DTG will often beat silk screen once the colour count climbs, because neither needs screens."]},{type:"h",text:"A rough rule"},{type:"p",text:"Polyester with complex artwork: sublimation. Cotton with one to three colours at volume: silk screen. Cotton with complex artwork or a short run: DTF or DTG. Mixed fabrics across one order: DTF, because it is the one method that behaves consistently on all of them."},{type:"p",text:"If you are unsure, send the artwork and the garment reference together. The two decide each other, and it is much cheaper to change method before sampling than after."}]},{slug:"what-happens-in-a-sampling-round",title:"What actually happens in a sampling round",date:"28 May 2026",iso:"2026-05-28",category:"Production",excerpt:"A walkthrough of what to check on your first sample, and the changes that are cheap to make before bulk.",readingTime:"5 min read",body:[{type:"p",text:"A sample is not a preview. It is the reference every piece in the bulk run gets measured against, which makes the approval the single most consequential decision in the whole order."},{type:"h",text:"What you receive"},{type:"p",text:"A first sample is usually made in one size — commonly M — in the actual fabric, with the actual decoration method, sewn on the same machines the bulk will run on. Where a fabric or trim is not yet available, a substitute may stand in, and that substitution should be stated rather than discovered."},{type:"h",text:"What to check, in order"},{type:"ul",items:["Measurements against the spec sheet, not against how it feels. Chest, length, sleeve, shoulder. A garment can fit well and still be off-spec, which becomes a problem when you grade it across sizes.","Colour under daylight, not office lighting. Screens and fluorescents both lie. If colour is critical, ask for a lab dip or a Pantone reference rather than approving from a photograph.","Decoration placement and size. Measure from the shoulder seam, not by eye.","Construction: seam type, stitch density, whether the neckband is taped, how the hem is finished.","Fabric hand and weight. This is your last chance to say it feels too thin."]},{type:"h",text:"Cheap to change now, expensive later"},{type:"p",text:"Before bulk, almost everything is adjustable: measurements, print position and scale, thread colour, label type and placement, packing method. These cost a revision round and some time."},{type:"p",text:"After cutting has started, the list shortens dramatically. Fabric and colour are effectively fixed, because the material has been bought and cut. Changing them means starting again."},{type:"note",text:'The most expensive sentence in apparel manufacturing is "can we just change the fabric?" said after cutting.'},{type:"h",text:"How many rounds is normal"},{type:"p",text:"One or two. A first sample plus one revision covers most straightforward garments. Three or more usually signals that the original spec was underdefined rather than that anything is going wrong — which is worth recognising early, because each round adds real time."},{type:"p",text:"Approve in writing, with the measurements attached. A verbal yes is not something either side can check against later."}]},{slug:"fabric-weights-explained",title:"Fabric weights, explained in plain terms",date:"09 May 2026",iso:"2026-05-09",category:"Materials",excerpt:"GSM, hand feel, and drape — what the numbers mean and how they translate into a finished garment.",readingTime:"4 min read",body:[{type:"p",text:"GSM stands for grams per square metre. It is a density measure, not a thickness one, and it is the single most useful number for predicting how a garment will feel before you can hold it."},{type:"h",text:"Typical ranges"},{type:"ul",items:["120–150 GSM — lightweight tees and performance jerseys. Breathable, quick-drying, and near the edge of being see-through in pale colours.","160–190 GSM — the standard retail t-shirt. Enough body to hang properly without being warm.","200–240 GSM — heavyweight tees and lighter sweatshirts. This is the range most streetwear brands mean when they say heavyweight.","280–350 GSM — hoodies and full-weight fleece. Substantial, warm, and noticeably more expensive to ship.","400 GSM and above — outerwear and premium heavyweight pieces."]},{type:"h",text:"What the number does not tell you"},{type:"p",text:"Two fabrics at the same GSM can feel completely different. Construction matters as much as weight: a 180 GSM slub knit drapes loosely, while a 180 GSM interlock feels firm and structured. Fibre matters too — cotton at 200 GSM sits heavier on the body than polyester at the same weight."},{type:"note",text:"GSM tells you density. Knit structure tells you drape. Ask for both, and ask for a swatch."},{type:"h",text:"The trade-offs to plan around"},{type:"p",text:"Heavier fabric reads as higher quality to most buyers, holds shape better, and hides construction flaws. It also costs more per metre, costs more to ship, takes longer to dry, and is uncomfortable in humid climates — a genuine consideration for garments worn in South East Asia."},{type:"p",text:"Lighter fabric is cheaper and more comfortable in heat, but shows sloppy seams, can be transparent in white and pastels, and tends to lose shape faster through repeated washing."},{type:"h",text:"A practical approach"},{type:"p",text:"Decide how the garment will actually be worn before choosing a weight. A race jersey and a retail hoodie sit at opposite ends of this scale for good reason. Then ask for swatches at two adjacent weights and handle both — the difference between 180 and 200 GSM is obvious in the hand and invisible on a spec sheet."}]},{slug:"planning-a-first-apparel-run",title:"Planning a first apparel run as a new label",date:"21 Apr 2026",iso:"2026-04-21",category:"Brand Building",excerpt:"Sizing curves, quantity splits, and the budget decisions that matter most on a debut collection.",readingTime:"6 min read",body:[{type:"p",text:"First runs go wrong in predictable ways, and almost all of them trace back to decisions made before anything was cut. A little planning here is worth more than any amount of problem-solving later."},{type:"h",text:"Fewer styles, more depth"},{type:"p",text:"The most common first-run mistake is launching six styles in four colourways. That is twenty-four variants, each needing its own minimum, and it spreads a small budget so thin that nothing is available in the sizes people actually want."},{type:"p",text:"Two or three styles in one or two colourways will teach you more, cost less, and leave you with stock you can actually sell through."},{type:"h",text:"Sizing curves"},{type:"p",text:"Do not split sizes evenly. A flat distribution across S to XXL leaves you with unsold ends and sold-out middles. A more realistic starting curve for adult unisex apparel looks roughly like this:"},{type:"ul",items:["S — 15%","M — 30%","L — 30%","XL — 18%","XXL — 7%"]},{type:"p",text:"Adjust it to your market rather than treating it as fixed. Womenswear skews smaller, workwear and team apparel skew larger, and regional differences are significant."},{type:"h",text:"Understand what the minimum applies to"},{type:"p",text:"Minimum order quantity is usually quoted per style per colour, not per order. A 100-piece MOQ across two colours means 200 pieces, not 100 split between them. Fabric minimums can apply separately again, since fabric is bought by the roll."},{type:"note",text:"Ask explicitly: is the MOQ per order, per style, or per style per colour? The answer changes your budget by multiples."},{type:"h",text:"Where the budget actually goes"},{type:"p",text:"New labels routinely budget for garments and forget everything around them. Beyond the unit cost, plan for sampling (usually charged, sometimes credited against bulk), labels and hangtags, packaging, shipping and duties, and a contingency for a second sampling round."},{type:"p",text:"A useful discipline is to hold back roughly a fifth of the total budget until the sample is approved. Something almost always needs adjusting, and having the room to fix it properly is the difference between a good first run and a compromised one."},{type:"h",text:"Build in more time than feels necessary"},{type:"p",text:"Sampling, revision, approval, fabric sourcing, production, and finishing each take real time, and they run in sequence rather than in parallel. Working backwards from a launch date is the right instinct; assuming each stage takes the minimum is not."}]}];function s(e){return a.find(t=>t.slug===e)}export{a as A,s as g};
